00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:37.616
All right, hands up again if
you've at least played around

00:00:37.616 --> 00:00:39.940
with Kubernetes, maybe
taken a tutorial.

00:00:39.940 --> 00:00:40.930
All right.

00:00:40.930 --> 00:00:46.300
How about if you're using
Kubernetes on a daily basis.

00:00:46.300 --> 00:00:47.440
Great.

00:00:47.440 --> 00:00:50.681
So we've got the whole
range here today.

00:00:50.681 --> 00:00:52.930
We are going to be covering
some of the basic building

00:00:52.930 --> 00:00:55.360
blocks of developing
applications on Kubernetes.

00:00:55.360 --> 00:00:58.030
But there should also
be some new information

00:00:58.030 --> 00:01:00.520
for everyone about
how to take advantage

00:01:00.520 --> 00:01:03.160
of the new application resource
that we've been working on

00:01:03.160 --> 00:01:06.280
to think about
applications as a more

00:01:06.280 --> 00:01:08.833
holistic unit in Kubernetes.

00:01:12.910 --> 00:01:17.620
So with Kubernetes, you can
manage computing resources.

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:20.080
So that's about
scheduling containers

00:01:20.080 --> 00:01:23.320
onto a cluster of servers.

00:01:23.320 --> 00:01:25.360
But I think one of the
reasons that Kubernetes

00:01:25.360 --> 00:01:28.100
has been so successful is
it doesn't just do that.

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:31.090
It also helps you manage
other types of infrastructure,

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:33.920
specifically
networking and storage.

00:01:33.920 --> 00:01:37.701
So if you're building most kinds
of real world applications,

00:01:37.701 --> 00:01:39.700
you need to configure
some amount of networking,

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.587
for example, configuring
service discoveries so

00:01:42.587 --> 00:01:44.920
that as your containers are
moving around in the cluster

00:01:44.920 --> 00:01:47.620
they can find each
other, or load balancers,

00:01:47.620 --> 00:01:49.060
or firewall rules.

00:01:49.060 --> 00:01:52.360
On the storage side, this is
about where your application

00:01:52.360 --> 00:01:54.250
data lives at rest.

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:56.770
For performance reasons,
you might want that storage

00:01:56.770 --> 00:01:59.680
in a locally attached SSD.

00:01:59.680 --> 00:02:01.660
Or you might want it
on a network attached

00:02:01.660 --> 00:02:05.780
file system so that it can
be more dynamically accessed.

00:02:05.780 --> 00:02:08.487
So you need to be thinking
about all these things

00:02:08.487 --> 00:02:10.570
when you're developing
applications for the cloud.

00:02:10.570 --> 00:02:13.810
And Kubernetes has a
really nice construct

00:02:13.810 --> 00:02:18.490
for doing this, which we
call infrastructure as code.

00:02:18.490 --> 00:02:20.950
In this example, there's a
resource here called a pod.

00:02:20.950 --> 00:02:23.390
If you're not familiar
with that, that's OK.

00:02:23.390 --> 00:02:26.440
The important thing is this is
a simple configuration file,

00:02:26.440 --> 00:02:29.170
and it lives in source code.

00:02:29.170 --> 00:02:32.680
And so it's in source code along
with all the other application

00:02:32.680 --> 00:02:36.067
code that you're developing
for your application.

00:02:36.067 --> 00:02:38.650
And that means you can use all
of your existing source control

00:02:38.650 --> 00:02:42.460
tools for revision control,
and code review and release.

00:02:42.460 --> 00:02:45.170
And that's really great from
a management perspective.

00:02:45.170 --> 00:02:47.244
And if you need to be
controlling and networking

00:02:47.244 --> 00:02:48.910
storage infrastructure
at the same time,

00:02:48.910 --> 00:02:52.301
that's just more of these
configuration files.

00:02:52.301 --> 00:02:53.800
And then when you're
ready, when you

00:02:53.800 --> 00:02:56.950
want to push an instance of
your application to Kubernetes,

00:02:56.950 --> 00:02:59.050
you just upload the
config to Kubernetes,

00:02:59.050 --> 00:03:00.700
and it's going to
manage the underlying

00:03:00.700 --> 00:03:03.080
infrastructure for you.

00:03:03.080 --> 00:03:06.130
So these configs are in
the language of Kubernetes.

00:03:06.130 --> 00:03:09.070
And Kubernetes
knows how to manage

00:03:09.070 --> 00:03:12.190
underlying infrastructure
wherever that might be--

00:03:12.190 --> 00:03:14.870
on Google Cloud, or
On-Prem, or somewhere else.

00:03:14.870 --> 00:03:17.110
And so that means as an
application developer,

00:03:17.110 --> 00:03:18.943
you don't actually have
to think about where

00:03:18.943 --> 00:03:21.370
your application is going
to be running in production.

00:03:21.370 --> 00:03:23.652
You can just target Kubernetes.

00:03:26.370 --> 00:03:29.560
So what about deploying
a whole application?

00:03:29.560 --> 00:03:31.507
What does that look like?

00:03:31.507 --> 00:03:33.840
So these days, there are
actually some pretty nice tools

00:03:33.840 --> 00:03:35.280
in the ecosystem for this.

00:03:35.280 --> 00:03:37.640
Helm is one such tool.

00:03:37.640 --> 00:03:40.830
And the Helm community has done
a really great job of packaging

00:03:40.830 --> 00:03:43.830
up many common open
source software stacks

00:03:43.830 --> 00:03:47.330
that you might want to
be using on Kubernetes.

00:03:47.330 --> 00:03:50.100
So with a command like
