00:04:41.050 --> 00:04:42.350
chances are it's not ready yet.

00:04:42.350 --> 00:04:44.670
There is some sort of
installation progress

00:04:44.670 --> 00:04:46.140
that needs to happen.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:50.934
Even in the cloud provisioning,
this infrastructure takes time.

00:04:50.934 --> 00:04:52.350
We do have that
list of resources,

00:04:52.350 --> 00:04:55.455
so what's their status?

00:04:55.455 --> 00:04:56.830
Once the thing is
up and running,

00:04:56.830 --> 00:04:58.210
you need to connect to it.

00:04:58.210 --> 00:05:00.570
So if it's a web
hosting application,

00:05:00.570 --> 00:05:04.770
that might mean connecting to
a URL for the site or the admin

00:05:04.770 --> 00:05:05.970
console.

00:05:05.970 --> 00:05:08.490
If it's a back end service,
you need a quick link

00:05:08.490 --> 00:05:11.970
to the service endpoint, the
credentials, maybe some client

00:05:11.970 --> 00:05:18.450
commands or tools for smoke
testing the application.

00:05:18.450 --> 00:05:21.360
And then that's really where
the process just begins.

00:05:21.360 --> 00:05:23.790
Because now you need to
customize the application

00:05:23.790 --> 00:05:25.387
for your use case.

00:05:25.387 --> 00:05:26.970
And for that, you
probably need a link

00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:30.870
to the user guide, maybe some
quick instructions for the most

00:05:30.870 --> 00:05:32.460
common operational tasks--

00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:37.850
like performing upgrades,
backups, restore, or deletion.

00:05:37.850 --> 00:05:39.600
So that's the general
problem that we're

00:05:39.600 --> 00:05:42.200
trying to tackle with
the application resource.

00:05:45.190 --> 00:05:47.070
So just a quick
note on the agenda.

00:05:47.070 --> 00:05:49.560
So we're going to introduce
the Kubernetes application

00:05:49.560 --> 00:05:51.030
resource.

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:53.610
We're going to talk about
how we've been integrating

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:57.450
applications into Google
Kubernetes engine, which

00:05:57.450 --> 00:06:01.560
is Google's hosted
Kubernetes offering.

00:06:01.560 --> 00:06:03.252
And we're also going
to talk about how

00:06:03.252 --> 00:06:04.710
you can get started
with these apps

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:09.040
quickly in the Google
Cloud platform marketplace.

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:11.040
So I will hand it
over to Ken Owens.

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:22.880
KENNETH OWENS: Thanks, David.

00:06:22.880 --> 00:06:23.477
Hi.

00:06:23.477 --> 00:06:24.060
I'm Ken Owens.

00:06:24.060 --> 00:06:27.222
I'm a software engineer on
Google Kubernetes engine.

00:06:27.222 --> 00:06:28.430
I work on the Workloads team.

00:06:28.430 --> 00:06:31.100
And in the open source community
a contributor and co-chair

00:06:31.100 --> 00:06:33.480
of Kubernetes SIG applications.

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.780
So as a contributor to SIG
applications, obviously

00:06:35.780 --> 00:06:37.670
I spend a lot of my time
thinking about apps.

00:06:41.310 --> 00:06:43.250
So to start with I
want to talk about some

00:06:43.250 --> 00:06:45.810
of the things that
go into applications.

00:06:45.810 --> 00:06:48.050
And from a show of
hands, most of you

00:06:48.050 --> 00:06:50.550
are familiar with Kubernetes.

00:06:50.550 --> 00:06:52.230
Can I see how many
people are using it

00:06:52.230 --> 00:06:55.160
in production right now?

00:06:55.160 --> 00:06:58.194
A substantial fraction,
so some of this

00:06:58.194 --> 00:06:59.610
might be a little
bit of overview.

00:06:59.610 --> 00:07:01.597
But for some of
you, this kind of

00:07:01.597 --> 00:07:03.930
is necessary to understand
what's going on with the demo

00:07:03.930 --> 00:07:06.330
later.

00:07:06.330 --> 00:07:06.900
So a pod.

00:07:06.900 --> 00:07:09.120
A pod is basically an
atom of scheduling.

00:07:09.120 --> 00:07:11.060
It's a set of
containers, some volumes,

00:07:11.060 --> 00:07:15.170
and the compute resources-- mem,
CPU-- to go along with that.

00:07:15.170 --> 00:07:18.320
Kubernetes is the
declarative API.

00:07:18.320 --> 00:07:21.230
So when you create a pod,
you specify those things.

00:07:21.230 --> 00:07:23.090
And the system goes
ahead and makes

00:07:23.090 --> 00:07:25.750
sure that pod gets scheduled and
create it as you specified it.

00:07:34.170 --> 00:07:38.010
So volumes-- volumes
are how your pods,

00:07:38.010 --> 00:07:40.870
if they have durable storage,
write things to disk.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:42.330
They mount inside
of the container

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:44.364
and it just appears
in the file system.

00:07:44.364 --> 00:07:45.780
Now, if you want
it to be durable.

00:07:45.780 --> 00:07:46.830
If it's not an
empty [? dir, ?] you

00:07:46.830 --> 00:07:48.390
don't want it to disappear--

00:07:48.390 --> 00:07:49.900
then you need to have a PV.

00:07:49.900 --> 00:07:51.540
PVs are persistent volume.

00:07:51.540 --> 00:07:54.360
On Google Cloud, that's backed
by Google Persistent Disk.

00:07:54.360 --> 00:07:56.790
PVs don't disappear
with the pod.

00:07:56.790 --> 00:07:59.040
If you want to schedule a
PV-- if you want to actually

00:07:59.040 --> 00:08:00.940
provision and mount
that into a pod,

00:08:00.940 --> 00:08:02.280
use a persistent volume claim.

00:08:05.762 --> 00:08:06.970
And all that's well and good.

00:08:06.970 --> 00:08:09.259
But ultimately, even if
you've got your compute,

00:08:09.259 --> 00:08:10.800
if you've got your
storage, if you've

00:08:10.800 --> 00:08:12.450
got your mem and
your CPU, you need

00:08:12.450 --> 00:08:14.605
to get network
traffic to the pod.

00:08:14.605 --> 00:08:16.230
In order to do that,
you use a service.

00:08:16.230 --> 00:08:18.540
This is basically your
atom of networking.

00:08:18.540 --> 00:08:21.180
So a service basically
just programs

00:08:21.180 --> 00:08:24.254
a network to route
traffic to a pod.

00:08:24.254 --> 00:08:25.920
And if you want to
expose it externally,

00:08:25.920 --> 00:08:27.510
you can do that on our Cloud.

00:08:27.510 --> 00:08:31.010
That will provision a
load balancer for you.

00:08:31.010 --> 00:08:33.309
So those basic core resources
are like the primitives

00:08:33.309 --> 00:08:35.482
that you use to turn up
a workload on Kubernetes.

00:08:35.482 --> 00:08:36.940
But they're not
really interesting.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:39.652
I mean, most workloads don't
consist of a single pod.

00:08:39.652 --> 00:08:41.860
And most people don't want
to go through the exercise

00:08:41.860 --> 00:08:44.054
of managing pods individually.

00:08:44.054 --> 00:08:45.720
That's where the
workloads APIs come in.

00:08:48.670 --> 00:08:50.140
So the most basic--

00:08:50.140 --> 00:08:52.540
and it's not really that
basic anymore-- resource

00:08:52.540 --> 00:08:55.780
for scheduling many pods are
deployments and replica sets.

00:08:55.780 --> 00:08:59.440
Now these are for your stateless
12-factor applications.

00:08:59.440 --> 00:09:01.540
When you create it, again,
it's a declarative API.

00:09:01.540 --> 00:09:02.230
You create a deployment.

00:09:02.230 --> 00:09:03.827
You say I want
n-pods, and I want

00:09:03.827 --> 00:09:04.993
them to have this container.

00:09:04.993 --> 00:09:06.937
I want them to have
this many resources.

00:09:06.937 --> 00:09:08.770
The system is going to
go ahead and schedule

00:09:08.770 --> 00:09:09.890
those pods for you.

00:09:09.890 --> 00:09:11.890
Now, it can roll them
out concurrently--

00:09:11.890 --> 00:09:13.760
meaning if you
create five pods, you

00:09:13.760 --> 00:09:15.010
may get two created at a time.

00:09:15.010 --> 00:09:17.230
If you scale that down--
if you say, you know what,

00:09:17.230 --> 00:09:18.840
I don't want five pods anymore--

00:09:18.840 --> 00:09:22.610
I'd like three pods, it may
kill two of them simultaneously.

00:09:22.610 --> 00:09:23.851
And that's fairly desirable.

00:09:23.851 --> 00:09:25.600
You want to be able
to accelerate rollouts

00:09:25.600 --> 00:09:27.580
for stateless
serving applications

00:09:27.580 --> 00:09:31.200
but not so much for databases.

00:09:31.200 --> 00:09:33.590
And that's where
StatefulSets come in.

00:09:33.590 --> 00:09:37.670
So StatefulSets are an attempt
to help you build applications,

00:09:37.670 --> 00:09:41.056
run workloads that contain
things like databases or other

00:09:41.056 --> 00:09:43.430
distributed storage systems,
or other distributed systems

00:09:43.430 --> 00:09:47.030
in general-- message queues,
Kafka, whatever you like--

00:09:47.030 --> 00:09:52.200
and we found that they required
some additional semantics

00:09:52.200 --> 00:09:52.700
around them.

00:09:52.700 --> 00:09:55.430
So for StatefulSets,
it'll do rollouts as well

00:09:55.430 --> 00:09:56.930
but it rolls them
out one at a time,

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:00.927
meaning that when you get pod 1,
pod 2 doesn't come up until pod

00:10:00.927 --> 00:10:02.720
1 says, OK, I'm ready.

00:10:02.720 --> 00:10:04.060
You can put traffic on me.

00:10:04.060 --> 00:10:06.950
When you tear it down,
you don't tear down pod 1

00:10:06.950 --> 00:10:09.470
until pod 2 says, OK, I'm done.

00:10:09.470 --> 00:10:11.986
Whatever I had to do to
clean up, it's happened.

00:10:11.986 --> 00:10:12.860
You can take me down.

00:10:12.860 --> 00:10:14.151
You can move on to my next guy.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:19.050
And other than
that, the pods also

00:10:19.050 --> 00:10:22.500
have durable provision storage
and a durable network identity.

00:10:22.500 --> 00:10:25.170
And what that means is
let's say a pod dies.

00:10:25.170 --> 00:10:27.900
When the pod comes back, it's
going to have the same network

00:10:27.900 --> 00:10:29.410
identity that it had before.

00:10:29.410 --> 00:10:32.230
And that's in terms of DNS--
the IP address may change.

00:10:32.230 --> 00:10:34.470
But again, using DNS,
it's generally enough

00:10:34.470 --> 00:10:37.080
to be able to identify
that pod correctly.

00:10:37.080 --> 00:10:38.580
More importantly,
potentially, it's

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:40.090
going to have the same storage.

00:10:40.090 --> 00:10:42.500
So if you wrote
something to a disk,

00:10:42.500 --> 00:10:43.972
pod 1's disk, when
it comes back,

00:10:43.972 --> 00:10:45.180
is going to be the same disk.

00:10:45.180 --> 00:10:47.055
And it will be able to
retrieve that storage.

00:10:49.200 --> 00:10:51.090
And lastly, you've
got DaemonSets.

00:10:51.090 --> 00:10:54.720
So some very complicated
apps, like, say,

00:10:54.720 --> 00:10:56.490
distributed file
systems, might want

00:10:56.490 --> 00:10:59.970
to do something
like deploy a pod

00:10:59.970 --> 00:11:01.780
to every node in the cluster.

00:11:01.780 --> 00:11:05.310
So if you're familiar with HDFS,
and you're deploying a data

00:11:05.310 --> 00:11:07.340
node, you want to select
the subset of nodes

00:11:07.340 --> 00:11:09.300
and say I want a data
node on all of these.

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:10.470
It's kind of a
different paradigm

00:11:10.470 --> 00:11:12.470
from just replication,
which is what deployments

00:11:12.470 --> 00:11:14.040
and StatefulSets are good at.

00:11:14.040 --> 00:11:16.534
And DaemonSets
are good for that.

00:11:16.534 --> 00:11:18.450
So now we've talked about
all these resources.

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:21.690
But David kind of made
it very explicit before.

00:11:21.690 --> 00:11:23.400
This is kind of hard to manage.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:25.720
you take all of these things
and plug them together,

00:11:25.720 --> 00:11:28.140
and at the end of
the day, it's really

00:11:28.140 --> 00:11:30.930
kind of hard to have
a unified view of what

00:11:30.930 --> 00:11:33.350
your application actually is.

00:11:33.350 --> 00:11:36.600
Moreover, all the tools
in the ecosystem kind of

00:11:36.600 --> 00:11:39.723
have a slightly different
definition of an application.

00:11:43.460 --> 00:11:44.770
Sorry, I missed this.

00:11:44.770 --> 00:11:48.010
ConfigMaps and Secrets are also
something you see commonly.

00:11:48.010 --> 00:11:51.850
They're just basically storage
for your configuration values,

00:11:51.850 --> 00:11:53.729
map of keys to a map values.

00:11:53.729 --> 00:11:55.770
And the difference between
ConfigMaps and Secrets

00:11:55.770 --> 00:11:57.820
is primarily that
Secrets are encrypted.

00:11:57.820 --> 00:12:00.250
And you also might see
some Ingresses later.

00:12:00.250 --> 00:12:05.420
Ingresses effectively route
traffic into the cluster.

00:12:05.420 --> 00:12:07.400
And they're used
for firewall rules.

00:12:07.400 --> 00:12:11.680
They'll do TLS termination,
and they work at layer 7.

00:12:11.680 --> 00:12:14.290
So back to what I
was saying before.

00:12:14.290 --> 00:12:17.650
So in SIG apps we
started thinking about,

00:12:17.650 --> 00:12:19.460
how can we defragment this?

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:22.360
How can we make sure that
ksonnet and Helm both look

00:12:22.360 --> 00:12:25.450
at an app the same way, and that
the GKE UI, and the Tectonic

00:12:25.450 --> 00:12:28.870
UI, and the OpenShift
UI all start looking

00:12:28.870 --> 00:12:31.310
at an app in the same way?

00:12:31.310 --> 00:12:34.320
So all the resources that
we've talked about before,

00:12:34.320 --> 00:12:36.320
there are nothing special.

00:12:36.320 --> 00:12:38.260
They ship with
Kubernetes by default.

00:12:38.260 --> 00:12:41.030
But Kubernetes provides you a
mechanism to extend the API,

00:12:41.030 --> 00:12:42.740
to have your own resources.

00:12:42.740 --> 00:12:46.730
And those resources aren't any
different in any meaningful way

00:12:46.730 --> 00:12:50.220
from the resources that we
ship with a distribution.

00:12:50.220 --> 00:12:52.400
So SIG apps sat around
and said, OK, well, let's

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:55.520
come up with a resource that
represents an application.

00:12:55.520 --> 00:12:57.120
Let's figure out
what that looks like.

00:12:57.120 --> 00:12:59.330
And let's do it with the
consensus of the community

00:12:59.330 --> 00:13:01.460
so that we don't
have 20 of them.

00:13:01.460 --> 00:13:02.120
We have one.

00:13:02.120 --> 00:13:05.390
Now, we're not going to ship
it by default with Kubernetes.

00:13:05.390 --> 00:13:07.730
But anybody who wants to use it.

00:13:07.730 --> 00:13:09.620
And primarily in
SIG apps, we have

00:13:09.620 --> 00:13:13.122
a lot of tool vendors, tool
builders and UI designers,

00:13:13.122 --> 00:13:14.580
they will be able
to use it, and we

00:13:14.580 --> 00:13:16.954
don't have to reinvent the
wheel from scratch every time.

00:13:16.954 --> 00:13:18.750
So it should promote
interoperability

00:13:18.750 --> 00:13:20.749
and reduce the level of
effort that developers

00:13:20.749 --> 00:13:22.290
have to put into
thinking about this.

00:13:24.960 --> 00:13:28.570
So what it really does is
it just groups resources,

00:13:28.570 --> 00:13:30.560
describes the
application, and provides

00:13:30.560 --> 00:13:33.112
a set of properties about
the application that

00:13:33.112 --> 00:13:34.820
are configurable on
a per-instance basis.

00:13:37.970 --> 00:13:40.130
So the resource metadata
is just the same metadata

00:13:40.130 --> 00:13:42.950
that you see with every
Kubernetes object.

00:13:42.950 --> 00:13:43.700
Pods have them.

00:13:43.700 --> 00:13:44.990
StatefulSets have them.

00:13:44.990 --> 00:13:48.380
It basically just identifies
the metadata of the application

00:13:48.380 --> 00:13:49.580
itself in of that instance.

00:13:53.550 --> 00:13:56.970
So the descriptor-- the
descriptor group's information

00:13:56.970 --> 00:14:00.210
about the application kind,
I guess you would say.

00:14:00.210 --> 00:14:02.470
So let's say you have
a MySQL application.

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:04.320
The descriptor
for MySQL probably

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:05.850
isn't going to change much.

00:14:05.850 --> 00:14:08.130
It contains things like
links to documentation,

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:09.930
the maintainers of
the application,

00:14:09.930 --> 00:14:12.330
or the maintainers of
the underlying code,

00:14:12.330 --> 00:14:14.297
some notes about
the application.

00:14:17.280 --> 00:14:20.430
Then with info
items-- so info items

00:14:20.430 --> 00:14:24.340
are individual to a specific
instance of the application.

00:14:24.340 --> 00:14:27.120
So this has things like
the administrative password

00:14:27.120 --> 00:14:30.930
for the app, a reference
to secrets, which

00:14:30.930 --> 00:14:35.100
is the same thing, IP addresses,
credentials, endpoints,

00:14:35.100 --> 00:14:37.590
and other config values that
are specific to an instance

00:14:37.590 --> 00:14:38.788
of the application.

00:14:42.910 --> 00:14:45.430
And lastly, this is
kind of the magic part,

00:14:45.430 --> 00:14:48.170
is the grouping of the
individual resources.

00:14:48.170 --> 00:14:52.580
So what this is actually saying
is OK, I produce StatefulSets.

00:14:52.580 --> 00:14:54.480
That combined with the
label selector that's

00:14:54.480 --> 00:14:57.240
in the application
allows any UI, any tool,

00:14:57.240 --> 00:14:59.790
to be able to go find
all the things that

00:14:59.790 --> 00:15:01.380
are in that application.

00:15:01.380 --> 00:15:03.990
And you'll see this in the
demo that's coming up next.

00:15:03.990 --> 00:15:06.660
It allows you to build
UIs consistently--

00:15:06.660 --> 00:15:08.880
allows anyone to build UIs--

00:15:08.880 --> 00:15:11.539
that are able to discover
all of the things that

00:15:11.539 --> 00:15:13.080
are in an application,
and present it

00:15:13.080 --> 00:15:15.180
to you in a way
that's meaningful and

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:16.895
application-specific.

00:15:20.272 --> 00:15:22.480
And next time we get a demo
from my colleague Konrad.

