00:20:47.580 --> 00:20:48.760
OK, back to slides, please.

00:20:48.760 --> 00:20:49.260
Thank you.

00:20:52.380 --> 00:20:54.976
So David mentioned Helm before.

00:20:54.976 --> 00:20:57.930
And you might be wondering
how this relates with Helm.

00:20:57.930 --> 00:20:59.880
And the answer is
it's fully compatible.

00:20:59.880 --> 00:21:03.250
It is just another
Kubernetes resource kind.

00:21:03.250 --> 00:21:09.170
So if we want to make use of
it, we just extend our Helm

00:21:09.170 --> 00:21:12.570
charts-- this is an example
list file for an example Helm

00:21:12.570 --> 00:21:13.410
chart--

00:21:13.410 --> 00:21:16.480
with a template for
generating applications.

00:21:16.480 --> 00:21:21.150
And then the installation
changes from this to that.

00:21:21.150 --> 00:21:23.940
And you just notice that
there is this new application

00:21:23.940 --> 00:21:26.100
resource getting deployed.

00:21:26.100 --> 00:21:30.780
So after you run this, you
will get this useful overview

00:21:30.780 --> 00:21:36.880
of your application for your
applications just like that.

00:21:36.880 --> 00:21:41.490
So if you are the only
author of your application

00:21:41.490 --> 00:21:44.070
and you have two
workloads running there,

00:21:44.070 --> 00:21:47.160
this is nice to have really.

00:21:47.160 --> 00:21:48.660
But it really comes
handy when there

00:21:48.660 --> 00:21:50.910
are several teams
in your company

00:21:50.910 --> 00:21:53.664
and you want to pass
information between each other.

00:21:53.664 --> 00:21:55.830
And it becomes really
critical when your application

00:21:55.830 --> 00:21:57.180
come from third party.

00:21:57.180 --> 00:22:00.700
So to tell you
more about that, I

00:22:00.700 --> 00:22:03.250
will pass the mic back to
David, who will tell you

00:22:03.250 --> 00:22:04.660
about Google Cloud Marketplace.

00:22:10.372 --> 00:22:13.710
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:13.710 --> 00:22:15.500
DAVID EUSTIS: Thinks, Konrad.

00:22:15.500 --> 00:22:18.000
So yeah, now that we've seen a
little bit about the building

00:22:18.000 --> 00:22:20.840
blocks of applications
and the new application

00:22:20.840 --> 00:22:23.280
resource and its
capabilities, I want

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:24.960
to show you what's
probably the quickest

00:22:24.960 --> 00:22:28.170
way to get started with
applications using the Google

00:22:28.170 --> 00:22:31.150
Cloud Platform Marketplace.

00:22:31.150 --> 00:22:33.486
If you're familiar
with GCP Marketplace,

00:22:33.486 --> 00:22:34.860
this is a product
that's actually

00:22:34.860 --> 00:22:36.390
been around for several years.

00:22:36.390 --> 00:22:39.420
It was formerly
called Cloud Launcher.

00:22:39.420 --> 00:22:41.790
And what you'll find
there is a catalog

00:22:41.790 --> 00:22:44.130
with literally
hundreds of solutions

00:22:44.130 --> 00:22:48.660
for whatever kind of problem
you're trying to solve on GCP.

00:22:48.660 --> 00:22:51.310
All of these solutions have
been vetted and tested by us.

00:22:51.310 --> 00:22:55.230
And we've made an effort to
make sure you can get started

00:22:55.230 --> 00:22:57.450
with these quickly, usually
just with a few clicks

00:22:57.450 --> 00:22:59.490
through the console.

00:22:59.490 --> 00:23:02.910
Some of these solutions
are built by Google.

00:23:02.910 --> 00:23:06.330
Those are usually the
open source stacks

00:23:06.330 --> 00:23:07.470
that are really popular.

00:23:07.470 --> 00:23:09.750
And we just want GCP
customers to have

00:23:09.750 --> 00:23:12.045
a great experience using those.

00:23:12.045 --> 00:23:13.920
But actually, the majority
of these solutions

00:23:13.920 --> 00:23:16.770
come from our third
party partners.

00:23:16.770 --> 00:23:19.680
And we have contracts
with all of these partners

00:23:19.680 --> 00:23:23.410
to provide long term maintenance
and support of these solutions.

00:23:23.410 --> 00:23:27.340
So again, if you're finding
solutions in the marketplace,

00:23:27.340 --> 00:23:30.810
you're going to have a
good time using those.

00:23:30.810 --> 00:23:33.120
Traditionally, we've
mostly had two types

00:23:33.120 --> 00:23:35.800
of products in the marketplace.

00:23:35.800 --> 00:23:38.580
One based on virtual
machines, so partners provide

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:39.894
virtual machine images--

00:23:39.894 --> 00:23:41.310
Deployment Manager
configurations,

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:43.110
if you're familiar with that.

00:23:43.110 --> 00:23:47.260
We also have some managed
services in the marketplace.

00:23:47.260 --> 00:23:50.100
But we're excited to have
announced just this past week

00:23:50.100 --> 00:23:53.670
that we're adding first
class support for Kubernetes

00:23:53.670 --> 00:23:54.930
in the marketplace.

00:23:54.930 --> 00:23:57.960
And we're actually the first
public cloud marketplace

00:23:57.960 --> 00:24:00.426
to have this capability.

00:24:00.426 --> 00:24:02.886
[APPLAUSE]

00:24:06.340 --> 00:24:09.280
But backing up for a
second, why would we

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:10.741
do this in Marketplace?

00:24:10.741 --> 00:24:12.490
I mean, we already
have all these hundreds

00:24:12.490 --> 00:24:16.780
of virtual machine solutions,
and they work great.

00:24:16.780 --> 00:24:19.660
But we actually think
there are some reasons

00:24:19.660 --> 00:24:22.390
that containers in
Kubernetes are a better way

00:24:22.390 --> 00:24:25.210
to distribute software,
especially package software

00:24:25.210 --> 00:24:27.407
and commercial software.

00:24:27.407 --> 00:24:29.740
So if you're a software vendor
out there in the audience

00:24:29.740 --> 00:24:32.520
or an enterprise consuming
commercial software,

00:24:32.520 --> 00:24:34.750
this part's for you
and take notice,

00:24:34.750 --> 00:24:38.680
because I think this is going
to be an important trend.

00:24:38.680 --> 00:24:41.740
So first of all, we all know
that containers are portable.

00:24:41.740 --> 00:24:45.580
So if you find a virtual machine
image from our marketplace,

00:24:45.580 --> 00:24:47.684
and then you try and
install it on prem,

00:24:47.684 --> 00:24:49.850
you're probably going to
have a hard time with that.

00:24:49.850 --> 00:24:52.390
But that's exactly
what containers

00:24:52.390 --> 00:24:53.830
would allow you to do.

00:24:53.830 --> 00:24:58.150
And similarly for Kubernetes,
like I mentioned-- when you're

00:24:58.150 --> 00:25:02.110
building an application
for Kubernetes,

00:25:02.110 --> 00:25:03.580
you don't need to
worry necessarily

00:25:03.580 --> 00:25:05.441
about where that
infrastructure--

00:25:05.441 --> 00:25:06.940
where the application
is going to be

00:25:06.940 --> 00:25:08.189
running at the end of the day.

00:25:08.189 --> 00:25:09.236
So that's also portable.

00:25:09.236 --> 00:25:11.110
So if you think about
that for a moment, that

00:25:11.110 --> 00:25:15.580
means if you find one of these
solutions in the marketplace,

00:25:15.580 --> 00:25:17.860
you can deploy it
onto Google Cloud.

00:25:17.860 --> 00:25:19.810
But you can also take
those exact same assets

00:25:19.810 --> 00:25:23.110
and deploy it onto Kubernetes,
wherever Kubernetes is running.

00:25:25.860 --> 00:25:29.220
Containers are also much lighter
weight than virtual machines.

00:25:29.220 --> 00:25:32.310
So a typical container image
might be a few megabytes

00:25:32.310 --> 00:25:33.310
for virtual machines.

00:25:33.310 --> 00:25:34.650
That's like gigabytes.

00:25:34.650 --> 00:25:36.150
And this makes a
big difference when

00:25:36.150 --> 00:25:38.290
you're trying to
rapidly scale up,

00:25:38.290 --> 00:25:42.360
scale down to
respond to traffic.

00:25:42.360 --> 00:25:44.490
It's also great from
the commercial vendor's

00:25:44.490 --> 00:25:46.470
perspective,
because what they're

00:25:46.470 --> 00:25:48.300
shipping to the
customer is really just

00:25:48.300 --> 00:25:50.400
their own application binary--

00:25:50.400 --> 00:25:52.620
it's immediate
runtime dependencies--

00:25:52.620 --> 00:25:54.540
not the whole operating system.

00:25:54.540 --> 00:25:57.390
And that's also great for
Marketplace customers,

00:25:57.390 --> 00:25:59.907
because they probably don't
want that operating system

00:25:59.907 --> 00:26:00.990
in their production fleet.

00:26:00.990 --> 00:26:05.070
They want to be using their own
golden vetted host operating

00:26:05.070 --> 00:26:09.840
system that their IT
department has given them.

00:26:09.840 --> 00:26:12.760
And then of course, we've seen,
with all these great primitives

00:26:12.760 --> 00:26:15.420
in Kubernetes that containers
have a lot of built

00:26:15.420 --> 00:26:17.880
in management
functionality for scaling,

00:26:17.880 --> 00:26:21.940
for rollouts, for
failure recovery.

00:26:21.940 --> 00:26:24.930
It's just much easier to
manage lots of these containers

00:26:24.930 --> 00:26:27.420
with Kubernetes than it
is to do the same thing

00:26:27.420 --> 00:26:28.570
with virtual machines.

00:26:31.970 --> 00:26:34.330
And since this is
the Marketplace,

00:26:34.330 --> 00:26:38.510
and we do have Enterprise-grade
commercial solutions,

00:26:38.510 --> 00:26:41.480
I want to mention that some of
these Kubernetes applications

00:26:41.480 --> 00:26:43.610
have integrated billing.

00:26:43.610 --> 00:26:44.670
So what does that mean?

00:26:44.670 --> 00:26:48.590
First of all, it's pay as you
go pricing, which is what you'd

00:26:48.590 --> 00:26:50.240
be familiar with in the cloud.

00:26:50.240 --> 00:26:52.260
That just means, the more
you use the something,

00:26:52.260 --> 00:26:53.080
the more you pay.

00:26:53.080 --> 00:26:54.746
If you shut it down
entirely, you're not

00:26:54.746 --> 00:26:56.210
going to pay anything anymore.

00:26:56.210 --> 00:26:58.230
You're going to get
one bill from Google.

00:26:58.230 --> 00:26:59.830
So as an Enterprise
customer, you

00:26:59.830 --> 00:27:02.840
don't need to have a
commercial Enterprise agreement

00:27:02.840 --> 00:27:04.070
with each of these vendors.

00:27:04.070 --> 00:27:05.570
We've done that work for you.

00:27:05.570 --> 00:27:09.260
And the fees will just show up
on your Google Cloud Platform

00:27:09.260 --> 00:27:11.815
bill.

00:27:11.815 --> 00:27:13.940
One thing that we've done
that's really interesting

00:27:13.940 --> 00:27:17.090
with these applications is
we're able to support a custom

00:27:17.090 --> 00:27:18.470
pricing metrics.

00:27:18.470 --> 00:27:21.020
So that means the pricing
for the application

00:27:21.020 --> 00:27:23.580
is really tailored
to the application.

00:27:23.580 --> 00:27:27.240
So for example, if it's
a database application,

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:29.120
the pricing might
be based on storage.

00:27:29.120 --> 00:27:31.820
Or if it's a CI/CD
application, the pricing

00:27:31.820 --> 00:27:33.560
might be based on
the number of builds

00:27:33.560 --> 00:27:35.340
or the number of deployments.

00:27:35.340 --> 00:27:38.720
And this works a lot
better than trying

00:27:38.720 --> 00:27:41.150
to squeeze all of these
commercial applications

00:27:41.150 --> 00:27:45.785
into a one size fits all billing
metric like core hours, which

00:27:45.785 --> 00:27:48.950
is what we've traditionally
used for virtual machines.

00:27:51.699 --> 00:27:53.740
And then importantly,
because we want all of this

00:27:53.740 --> 00:27:56.290
to be portable,
the metering system

00:27:56.290 --> 00:27:58.540
that we've designed for
this is cloud agnostic.

00:27:58.540 --> 00:28:00.640
And that means the
metering will happen

00:28:00.640 --> 00:28:03.100
whether the containers
are running on GCP

00:28:03.100 --> 00:28:05.410
or whether they're
running somewhere else.

00:28:05.410 --> 00:28:07.650
To show you a little
bit how that works--

00:28:07.650 --> 00:28:10.630
essentially the
applications themselves

00:28:10.630 --> 00:28:12.880
are generating billing metrics.

00:28:12.880 --> 00:28:15.760
They're reporting those
metrics to a Google managed

00:28:15.760 --> 00:28:18.580
reporting end point, and
identifying the customer

00:28:18.580 --> 00:28:20.890
billing account when
they make those reports.

00:28:20.890 --> 00:28:23.230
And so then at the end of
the month, Google tallies up

00:28:23.230 --> 00:28:27.670
all the usage, bills the
customer, pays the vendor.

00:28:27.670 --> 00:28:30.200
And that's pretty
much how it works.

00:28:30.200 --> 00:28:35.350
So we're really excited to
have some of the top tier

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:36.850
third party software
vendors working

00:28:36.850 --> 00:28:39.950
with us for the introduction
of these commercial Kubernetes

00:28:39.950 --> 00:28:41.930
applications.

00:28:41.930 --> 00:28:44.950
Some of these names you
should be familiar with.

00:28:44.950 --> 00:28:48.520
You can see that this really
spans a wide range of computing

00:28:48.520 --> 00:28:55.600
categories, from databases to
security applications, CI/CD

00:28:55.600 --> 00:28:56.890
logging, monitoring.

00:28:56.890 --> 00:28:58.600
I think this is
kind of a testament

00:28:58.600 --> 00:29:00.907
to the power of
Kubernetes itself

00:29:00.907 --> 00:29:03.490
to be able to support all these
different types of distributed

00:29:03.490 --> 00:29:04.660
workloads.

00:29:04.660 --> 00:29:08.950
And we're really excited to
have seen all the excitement

00:29:08.950 --> 00:29:10.180
from these vendors.

00:29:10.180 --> 00:29:14.230
I think they've understood
the new wave of containers

00:29:14.230 --> 00:29:17.290
in Kubernetes for commercial
software distribution.

00:29:17.290 --> 00:29:20.860
And it's come through here.

00:29:20.860 --> 00:29:24.030
So thanks to all
of our partners.

00:29:24.030 --> 00:29:28.070
So I'm going to jump
over to a demo real quick

00:29:28.070 --> 00:29:31.610
to show you how easy
it is to actually use

00:29:31.610 --> 00:29:33.980
these from the console.

00:29:33.980 --> 00:29:40.610
So here I am in the Google
Cloud Platform console UI.

00:29:40.610 --> 00:29:42.350
We're in the Kubernetes section.

00:29:42.350 --> 00:29:45.180
I have a couple of
clusters running here,

00:29:45.180 --> 00:29:48.870
but not much else is
running in this cluster.

00:29:48.870 --> 00:29:50.750
So I'll go over to the
applications section

00:29:50.750 --> 00:29:56.550
that Konrad was showing you and
click Deploy From Marketplace.

00:29:56.550 --> 00:30:01.430
And so right away, I've got a
catalog here with currently 27

00:30:01.430 --> 00:30:02.010
solutions.

00:30:02.010 --> 00:30:03.560
That will be growing
all the time.

00:30:03.560 --> 00:30:07.260
Different computing
categories are available here.

00:30:07.260 --> 00:30:11.450
If I open one of these--
for example Aerospike,

00:30:11.450 --> 00:30:15.680
Aerospike is a flash-optimized
NoSQL database.

00:30:15.680 --> 00:30:19.790
You can see in this case I've
already purchased a plan.

00:30:19.790 --> 00:30:23.280
The pricing is based
on storage hours.

00:30:32.420 --> 00:30:36.250
But I won't deploy
Aerospike right now.

00:30:36.250 --> 00:30:39.020
Let's go take a look-- we've got
some other great solutions here

00:30:39.020 --> 00:30:41.630
from Aqua.

00:30:41.630 --> 00:30:43.692
This is a security application.

00:30:43.692 --> 00:30:45.650
They have a couple of
different pricing metrics

00:30:45.650 --> 00:30:49.340
based on the size of the
nodes that are being monitored

00:30:49.340 --> 00:30:51.481
for this security application.

00:30:54.710 --> 00:30:58.800
But I think I will deploy
an instance of RabbitMQ.

00:30:58.800 --> 00:31:04.370
So that's a distributed
message queue system.

00:31:04.370 --> 00:31:07.790
This one happens to
be packaged by Google.

00:31:07.790 --> 00:31:09.230
And so it's free to use.

00:31:09.230 --> 00:31:12.178
You just pay for your
usage of Kubernetes.

00:31:16.250 --> 00:31:17.830
So if I go to Configure This--

00:31:21.890 --> 00:31:24.652
so right now it's got one
of my clusters selected.

00:31:24.652 --> 00:31:26.860
I could switch to a different
cluster if I wanted to,

00:31:26.860 --> 00:31:29.201
but I'll just keep
the defaults here.

00:31:29.201 --> 00:31:31.450
There's some custom properties
that I could configure.

00:31:31.450 --> 00:31:36.820
So if I want more replicas of
RabbitMQ, I could change this.

00:31:36.820 --> 00:31:38.845
If I want to change
the default user name,

00:31:38.845 --> 00:31:40.300
I can change that to.

00:31:40.300 --> 00:31:43.870
There are some quick links to
the User Guide Tutorials here.

00:31:43.870 --> 00:31:45.840
But let's just go
ahead and deploy this.

00:31:48.420 --> 00:31:51.470
So what's happening here
is we're essentially

00:31:51.470 --> 00:31:55.250
pushing that configuration
to the Kubernetes API,

00:31:55.250 --> 00:31:57.410
the same way you would
do it with a command line

00:31:57.410 --> 00:32:00.090
tool like Helm.

00:32:00.090 --> 00:32:04.710
At this point, all
the resources have

00:32:04.710 --> 00:32:07.230
been created in Kubernetes,
and it's just waiting

00:32:07.230 --> 00:32:08.620
for a few pods to be created.

00:32:08.620 --> 00:32:11.280
So I'm going to go ahead and
shortcut this and jump over

00:32:11.280 --> 00:32:15.450
to the in progress
installation here.

00:32:15.450 --> 00:32:18.190
So we can see some
components are still pending.

00:32:18.190 --> 00:32:21.846
But actually, it's just
waiting for a few pods

00:32:21.846 --> 00:32:23.220
to be created in
the StatefulSet.

00:32:23.220 --> 00:32:27.810
So things should be more or
less working now already.

00:32:27.810 --> 00:32:29.740
So just to show you
what we have here--

00:32:29.740 --> 00:32:33.940
so there's no
external IP address

00:32:33.940 --> 00:32:35.497
for this RabbitMQ cluster.

00:32:35.497 --> 00:32:36.830
And that's probably a good idea.

00:32:36.830 --> 00:32:39.520
You don't want to be exposing
your backend services

00:32:39.520 --> 00:32:42.250
to the internet if
you don't have to.

00:32:42.250 --> 00:32:45.230
But just to show you
that this is working,

00:32:45.230 --> 00:32:49.550
we can do a quick little
port forwarding here,

00:32:49.550 --> 00:32:52.390
assuming this works.

00:32:52.390 --> 00:32:57.850
So here is the service where the
admin console is being exposed.

00:32:57.850 --> 00:33:00.190
This is a neat trick
in the console.

00:33:00.190 --> 00:33:03.300
You can actually set up port
forwarding from your browser

00:33:03.300 --> 00:33:05.880
to an IP address
inside the cluster.

00:33:13.880 --> 00:33:14.930
Cross your fingers here.

00:33:23.460 --> 00:33:25.760
All right, so that's
the Admin console.

00:33:25.760 --> 00:33:28.520
But wait, I don't have
the username and password.

00:33:28.520 --> 00:33:32.660
But thankfully, I just go back
to the application resource

00:33:32.660 --> 00:33:34.280
and find them easily here.

00:33:34.280 --> 00:33:39.910
So the user name is rabbit, and
the password here is gibberish.

00:33:39.910 --> 00:33:43.225
We generated that so that it
would be secure out of the box.

00:33:48.584 --> 00:33:51.250
And I don't know why it's saying
that because the login actually

00:33:51.250 --> 00:33:52.420
succeeded.

00:33:52.420 --> 00:33:53.892
And here all the nodes.

00:33:53.892 --> 00:33:55.600
And there's nothing
running in the queue.

00:33:55.600 --> 00:33:58.784
But that's-- there you have it.

00:33:58.784 --> 00:34:01.676
[APPLAUSE]

00:34:04.770 --> 00:34:05.270
All right.

00:34:05.270 --> 00:34:09.190
Let's switch back
to the slides now.

00:34:09.190 --> 00:34:10.750
So at this point,
I was just going

00:34:10.750 --> 00:34:13.719
to take questions, if I could
invite Ken and Konrad back

00:34:13.719 --> 00:34:14.560
onto the stage.

